# fastlane app metadata for the iOS TestFlight pipeline.
#
# Both values are overridable from the environment so an Apple account change needs no code
# edit. CI feeds IOS_TEAM_ID from the `IOS_TEAM_ID` repository *variable* — a Team ID is an
# account identifier printed on every provisioning profile, not a credential, so it is
# deliberately not a secret.
#
# Only the main app identifier belongs here. The widget, the share extension, the watch app and
# the watch complication all ride along inside the same `Tday` archive; their bundle IDs come
# from the Xcode project, not from fastlane.

# Same rule as the lane's `env_or`: an environment variable set to the empty string is unset.
# GitHub Actions writes `IOS_TEAM_ID: ${{ vars.IOS_TEAM_ID }}` as "" when the variable is not
# defined, and Ruby's `||` treats "" as present — so a plain `ENV[...] || default` here would
# hand fastlane an empty value and silently drop the fallback below (AppfileConfig#setter
# discards blanks, so the key would end up simply absent while the Fastfile's `env_or` still
# resolved the default — the two would disagree about the team).
#
# A lambda rather than a `def`: this file is `eval`'d inside CredentialsManager::AppfileConfig,
# and it is re-evaluated on every `try_fetch_value`, so a top-level `def` would repeatedly
# define a method on that class. A local is scoped to this file and cannot leak.
tday_env_or = lambda do |name, fallback|
  value = ENV[name].to_s.strip
  value.empty? ? fallback : value
end

app_identifier(tday_env_or.call("IOS_APP_IDENTIFIER", "com.ohmz.tday.ios"))
team_id(tday_env_or.call("IOS_TEAM_ID", "JUFACN2FS3"))
